course: everyday
lesson: 439
title: Cruel pranks and where the line is
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Some kids in Year 5 told Callum there was a party at the park on Saturday. He went, with a present. Nobody came — they had made it up to laugh at him. Callum went home in tears, his mum told the school, and the head said: «That's not a prank. That's cruelty.» A prank is cruel when only one person gets hurt and everyone else laughs. That is bullying in a costume.
explain: A teacher in Cork gave her class three tests for any prank. Does everyone laugh, including the one pranked? Can it be undone easily? Would you be happy if it was done to you? The class tried them on ten real pranks, and half failed. Big kids in Glasgow sent wee Lewis to ask the shopkeeper for «a tin of tartan paint»; there is no such thing, the shopkeeper played along, and Lewis laughed too when he got it — next year he sent a younger kid for «a long stand». That old joke passes the tests. Many others don't.
explain: Bodies are not toys. Jake saw a chair-pulling prank in a funny video and pulled the chair away as his mate sat down; his mate hit his head on the floor and had to go to hospital for a check. «That's why it's not funny in real life,» said Jake's mum; prank videos are often set up. Ellie put salt in Olivia's water bottle; Olivia drank a lot before PE, felt sick and had to sit out. Sam told his little cousin that eating a bug would give him a superpower; the cousin ate a woodlouse and cried, and his aunt was raging. Tricking someone smaller into doing something gross or risky is using their trust.
explain: Some pranks hurt feelings that don't show. Girls in Leeds sent Mia a fake love note «from» a boy, then read her reply aloud to the class; Mia stayed off school for two days, and it hurt for ages. Mason's mates hid his bag every day for a week «as a joke»; he laughed along, then cried every night, until his mum noticed. «We didn't know he minded,» said the mates. If it is always the same target, it is not a joke — watch for fake laughs.
explain: Pranks can frighten strangers. Lads in Sydney filmed themselves «pranking» a shop worker by pretending to steal and running; they posted it, the worker was shaking, and when their mums saw it they had to go back and apologise in person. «I didn't think about her at all,» one said. Emma was dared to prank-call a random number; a grandma answered, frightened it was bad news about her grandson. Emma hung up, felt awful and told her mum: «You never know who's on the other end.»
fact: A prank is cruel when only one person is hurt or humiliated and the others laugh; that is bullying.
fact: Three tests for a prank: everyone laughs, including the pranked one; it can be undone easily; you'd be happy if it were done to you.
fact: If a prank fails any of the three tests, don't do it.
fact: Pulling a chair away as someone sits can cause a fall and a head injury.
fact: Prank videos online are often staged; real falls break bones and hurt heads.
fact: Putting anything into someone's food or drink without them knowing is not a joke; it could make them ill.
fact: Tricking a younger child into eating something gross or doing something risky is using their trust.
fact: Pranks that trick someone into sharing feelings and then laugh at them are deeply cruel.
fact: If the same person is always the target, it is picking on them, even if they laugh along.
fact: «It's just a prank» does not make it okay if the person is scared or humiliated.
fact: Prank calls and fake-crime pranks frighten real people who don't know it's a joke.
fact: Some old fool's-errand jokes, like «tartan paint», are a harmless tradition when the person laughs in the end.
fact: If you see a cruel prank, don't join in, and tell a grown-up.
exercise: What did the kids tell Callum? | That there was a party at the park on Saturday | That school was closed on Monday for a snow day | That his bag had been thrown in the big bin | That a teacher wanted to see him
exercise: What did the head say about the fake party? | «That's not a prank. That's cruelty.» | «It's just a bit of fun, boys will be boys» | «Callum should have checked first» | «Parties are not allowed»
exercise: What happened to Jake's mate when the chair was pulled away? | He hit his head and went to hospital for a check | He landed softly on a cushion and laughed with everyone | He sat on the floor and cheered | Nothing, he saw it coming
exercise: Where had Jake seen the chair prank? | In a funny video | In the school play at Christmas | In a comic book | On a poster
exercise: What did the girls do with Mia's reply to the fake note? | Read it aloud to the whole class | Gave it straight back to Mia without reading it | Passed it on to the boy | Threw it away unread
exercise: What did Sam's little cousin do after hearing about the superpower? | Ate a woodlouse and cried | Climbed the tallest tree in the garden to fly | Asked his mum first | Laughed and said no
exercise: What did the lads in Sydney do with their shop prank video? | Posted it online | Deleted it at once | Gave it to the police | Showed it to the shop worker
exercise: What did the lads have to do when their mums saw it? | Go back and apologise in person | Pay for everything in the whole shop | Make another video saying sorry | Nothing at all
exercise: Why did Olivia have to sit out PE? | She drank salty water and felt sick | She forgot her PE kit at home again | She hurt her ankle | She was late
exercise: How did Mason really feel about his bag being hidden? | He cried every night | He thought it was hilarious, every time | He didn't notice it was gone | He hid their bags back
exercise: Who first noticed Mason was upset? | His mum | His mates | His teacher | His little sister
exercise: Who answered Emma's prank call? | A frightened grandma | A shop worker | A teacher from her school | A police officer
exercise: What did Emma's mum say? | «You never know who's on the other end» | «Prank calls are fine once, but not twice in a row» | «Call again to say sorry» | «Choose a better number»
exercise: What are the Cork teacher's three tests? | Everyone laughs, it can be undone, you'd be happy if done to you | It's quick, it's cheap, and nobody ever finds out who did it | It's online, it's funny, it gets views | It's loud, it's big, it's new
exercise: What is «tartan paint»? | A made-up thing for a harmless fool's-errand joke | A real paint they use to make kilts in Scotland | A Scottish sweet | A kind of wallpaper
exercise: Why does the tartan paint joke pass the tests? | Lewis laughed in the end and nothing was harmed | Lewis never found out it was a joke at all | The shopkeeper sold him paint | It cost Lewis money
test: Mates: «it's just a prank, bro, chill». The pranked kid is crying. What's true? | If someone is scared or hurt, it's not just a prank | Calling it a prank makes it fine, whatever happens next | Crying means it was funny | He needs to chill
test: Mason laughs every time his bag is hidden. Does that mean he's fine? | Not necessarily — fake laughs hide hurt | Yes, laughing means he loves it | Yes, he would always say if he minded it | No, he's just tired
test: Kid: «saw a chair prank on TikTok, everyone fell on cushions». What's true in real life? | Videos are often set up; real falls hurt heads | Everyone always lands softly, like in the videos | Chairs are made for pranks | It's safe if you're quick
test: Big kid: «eat this bug, you'll get superpowers». What's the right reply for the little one? | No, and tell a grown-up | Yes, superpowers sound great | Only if it's a small bug | Ask which superpower first
test: Lewis comes back from the shop puzzled, no tartan paint, and the big kids roar. Then Lewis laughs too. Which test does this pass? | Everyone laughs, including the pranked one | Nobody gets laughed at, not even a little | It can't be undone | Only the big kids laugh
test: Dare: «prank-call a random number, go on». What would happen if a scared old lady answered? | She might think something bad happened to her family | She'd laugh and call back | Nothing, because old people love getting any phone call | She'd thank you
test: Your friends plan a fake love note to a girl in class. What do you do? | Don't join in, and tell a grown-up | Help write it nicely so it's less mean | Watch but don't write | Tell the girl it's real
test: «put salt in her bottle lol». Why is this not a harmless joke? | Anything put in someone's drink secretly could make them ill | Salt is much too expensive to waste on a joke | She'd taste it at once | It's fine if she laughs
test: Lads fake-steal in a shop for a video. Worker's shaking. Which of the three tests does it fail? | Would you be happy if it was done to you? | None, it passes all three | Only «can it be undone?» | It's not a prank at all, so no tests apply
test: Callum went to the fake party with a present. What would make it right afterwards? | A real sorry and stopping it for good | Inviting him to another party, a real one this time, to make up | Saying it was only a joke | Laughing with him about it
test: In a cartoon, a character is pranked every episode and always smiles. What's closer to real life? | Being the target again and again hurts | Being pranked is always fun if you smile | Targets enjoy being picked | Cartoon pranks are safe to copy
exam: Identify the principal characteristic which distinguishes a cruel prank from a good-natured one. | One person is harmed or humiliated while others are amused | The prank is performed out of doors | The prank is elaborate in its preparation and requires several persons | The prank occurs on a weekday
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the removal of a chair as a person is about to sit. | It may occasion a fall and injury to the head | It is harmless, as demonstrated in prank videos | It is permissible among friends | It improves the balance of the subject
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the repeated concealment of one pupil's bag by others «as a joke»? | It constitutes picking on that pupil, notwithstanding that he laughs | It is harmless, the pupil having laughed on each occasion | It is a fair game in which all pupils participate equally | It is a matter of no concern to adults
exam: It is given that a prank fails one of the three tests stated by the teacher. Determine the appropriate course. | The prank ought not to be performed | The prank may be performed once only | The prank may be performed if it is filmed for others | The prank may be performed upon a younger child
